---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/btu';
import { ENERGY_STAR_BTU } from '../lib/hvac';

const rows = ENERGY_STAR_BTU.map(([max, btu], i) => ({ from: i === 0 ? 100 : ENERGY_STAR_BTU[i - 1][0], max, btu }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="btu-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="BTU Calculator: Air Conditioner Size for a Room"
  description="Free BTU calculator. Enter room size, sun exposure, occupants, and whether it's a kitchen to get the air conditioner BTUs and tons you need, using the ENERGY STAR sizing method."
  h1="BTU Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Size a window, portable, or mini-split air conditioner for a room using the ENERGY STAR method, with a rough heating
    estimate for your climate.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How many BTUs do I need?</h2>
  <p>
    Start from the room's square footage in the table below, then adjust: take off 10% for a heavily shaded room, add 10%
    for a very sunny one, add 600 BTU for each regular occupant beyond two, and add 4,000 BTU for a kitchen. A
    <strong>400 sq ft room with normal sun needs about 9,000 BTU</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Air conditioner size by room size</h2>
  <p>ENERGY STAR guidance for room air conditioners.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Room area</th><th class="num">Cooling capacity</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.from}–{r.max.toLocaleString('en-US')} sq ft</td><td class="num">{r.btu.toLocaleString('en-US')} BTU/h</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="tons">BTUs and tons</h2>
  <p>
    Central air and heat pumps are sold in tons. One ton of cooling is 12,000 BTU per hour, so a 24,000 BTU system is 2
    tons. Room units are usually labeled in BTU.
  </p>

  <h2 id="heating">How many BTUs to heat a room?</h2>
  <p>
    A rough rule is 30 to 60 BTU per square foot, higher in colder climates and poorly insulated homes. The calculator
    shows a range for the climate you pick. Treat it as a starting point for space heaters and small zones, not for a
    furnace.
  </p>

  <p>
    L-shaped room or open plan? <a href="/square-footage-calculator/">Add up the floor area</a> first and enter the total.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Is a bigger air conditioner better?</h3>
  <p>
    No. An oversized unit cools the air quickly and shuts off before it removes much humidity, leaving the room cold and
    damp, and it cycles more often. Choose the size that matches the room.
  </p>
  <h3>What about whole-house systems?</h3>
  <p>
    Central systems should be sized with a Manual J load calculation, which accounts for insulation, windows, air leakage,
    and orientation. A contractor can run one; square-footage rules often oversize.
  </p>
  <p>
    Improving the room instead? Better attic insulation cuts the load; see the
    <a href="/insulation-calculator/">insulation calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Base capacity from the ENERGY STAR room air conditioner sizing table; rooms over 2,500 sq ft are extrapolated.</li>
    <li>Adjustments: −10% shaded, +10% sunny, +600 BTU per person over two, +4,000 BTU for a kitchen.</li>
    <li>Heating range = area × 30–60 BTU per sq ft depending on climate. Rough estimate only.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
